1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,280
Malcolm X and Ho Chi Minh, two great revolutionaries who fought against colonialism and oppression,

2
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:10,480
were both born on 19 May. However, a closer look at their political history and ideas

3
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:16,880
reveal that they shared more than just a birthday. Malcolm X, born Malcolm Little on 19 May 1925,

4
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:22,160
in Omaha, Nebraska, was born into a political family. His parents, Earl and Luis Little,

5
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:26,640
were active members of the United Negro Improvement Association, or the UNIA,

6
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:31,920
an organization founded by the Honorable Marcus Mosiah Garvey. Marcus Garvey was one of the 20th

7
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:37,120
century's most prominent Pan-African leaders, and at its height, the UNIA had up to 6 million

8
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:42,480
members worldwide. Its members were Africans, whether on the continent or in the diaspora,

9
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:47,360
who believed that Africa belongs to African people, and that Africa must unite into one

10
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:52,480
Pan-African state known as the United States of Africa, so that all Africans in the diaspora,

11
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:57,440
dedicated to the cause, may one day return home and join their sisters and brothers on the continent.

12
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:02,320
Marcus Garvey had a tremendous impact on the Pan-African movement historically and into the present.

13
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:21,360
However, Africans were not the only ones inspired by Marcus Garvey's ideas. Ho Chi Minh, born on 19 May,

14
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:27,440
1890, in what was then the French colony of Indochina, also became a huge admirer of Marcus Garvey.

15
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:32,640
Marcus Garvey even played a role in his decision to fight for the liberation of his people in Vietnam.

16
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:38,320
In the 1920s, Ho Chi Minh was a student of Columbia University in New York City. As a working-class

17
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:44,160
person from a colonized background, he lived in and spent his time in Harlem, a massive hub of UNIA

18
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:49,600
activities. He reportedly attended many lectures given by Marcus Garvey during this time, shaping his early

19
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:54,480
political thought. He even wrote several essays that are now compiled in a collection called The

20
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:59,440
Black Race. The 20 essays in the book explain the history of oppression against black people,

21
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:04,160
from slavery to Jim Crow. Ultimately, Malcolm X and Ho Chi Minh were closely united in their

22
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:09,680
conviction that colonized and oppressed people must fight for national liberation. Malcolm X became one of

23
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:14,640
the first Pan-African voices to denounce U.S. intervention in Vietnam. He declared that the United States,

24
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:20,880
complete defeat in South Vietnam is only a matter of time, and said, I think young people here can

25
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:26,480
find the powerful example in the young Simbas in the Congo and the young fighters in South Vietnam.

